                      ALMOND MONITORING DAEMON SOURCE INSTALLATION

____________________________________________________________________________________________________

1. PREREQUISITES
   In order to run the install script you need to have a c compiler, make and automake installed.
   - gcc
   - make
   - automake

   Make sure you have the dependecies for the source installation, meaning headers for:
   - json-c
   - ssl
   - rdkafka
   - libjwt

   For the default plugins to work you also need to have the following installed:
   - ksh
   - sysstat
   - python3
   - perl
   - python3-psutil (pip3 install psutil)
   - ping
   - ps

2. RUN THE INSTALL SCRIPT
   Run the script ./install_almond.sh

   This script will compile and install Almond monitoring daemon and copy initial configuration and
   plugin files.

   If you want to compile yourself take note below:
   To compile almond with support for kafka_export, make sure you have the correct dependecies installed and run:
   gcc -g -Wall main.c api.c config.c logger.c plugins.c mod_kafka.c -pthread -L/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu -I/usr/include/json_c -I/usr/include/librdkafka -std=c99 -O0 -ljson-c -lrdkafka -lssl -ljwt -lcrypto -lz -fstack-protector -o almond

   To compile with Avro support for Kafka, you need to install required dependecies and edit the schema registry path in the mod_avro.c file. You also need to rename mod_avro.c and mod_avro.h to mod_kafka.c and mod_kafka.h, overwriting existing files and then compile with
   gcc -g -Wall main.c api.c config.c logger.c plugins.c mod_avro.c -pthread -L/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu -I/usr/include/json_c -I/usr/include/librdkafka -I /usr/include/avro -I /usr/include/libserdes -std=c99 -O0 -ljson-c -lserdes -lavro -lrdkafka -lssl -ljwt -lcrypto -lz -fstack-protector -o almond
   Also make sure to register the schema used by the Almond Kafka producer:
   curl -X POST http://localhost:18081/subjects/almond-monitor-topic-value/versions \
     -H "Content-Type: application/vnd.schemaregistry.v1+json" \
     -d @<(echo '{"schema": '"$(jq -Rs . < plugins_status.avsc)"'}')
   Also take note that this schema requires you to use kafkaStartId and kafkaTag meta data options.

   To just compile almond without Kafka you need to remove Kafka references in the source code then you write:
   gcc -g -Wall main_s.c api_s.c config_s.c logger.c plugins.c  -pthread -L/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu -I/usr/include/json_c -std=c99 -O0 -ljson-c  -lssl -ljwt -lcrypto -lz -fstack-protector -o almond

3. START THE ALMOND PROCESS
   After running the install script you can start Almond with the following command:
   
   /opt/almond/start_almond.sh

4. INSTALLING WITH AUTOMAKE
   Make sure everyting is clean between builds, running autoreconf -fi
   Other than that is just to run the regular configure, make, make install commands.
   Configure can be run in three ways:
   ./configure --prefix=/opt/almond		 	standard installation of Almond with Kafka support
   ./configure --prefix=/opt/almond --enable-avro	installs Almond with avro support for Kafka
   ./configure --prefix=/opt/almond --disable-kafka	installs a minimal Almond without support for Kafka

5. PLUGIN NOTICE
   Some plugins installed by default will have their own prerequisites such as Python and Perl with
   additional modules. Some plugins are binary and compiled under x86_64/amd64 for Debian and if
   they are not working you need to download/install/compile them for the distribution and or
   architecture where you want to run the Almond software.
